# The firmware's NHLT for the driver VM: an Intel audio controller's table
# of its DSP's links (the digital microphones and ports on them), without
# which Linux's SOF does not find the microphones. It goes to the guest
# with the controller: QEMU's Intel HD Audio controller, and a test's NHLT
# (with no links) added to QEMU's tables. The guest's tables have it as the
# firmware does, after the FADT, and Linux finds it there.
acpi-table nhlt
sound hda
boot-cmdline "drivervm.poweroff"
fail-on "PANIC"
fail-on "drivervm: processor"
wait-serial "comes with the firmware's NHLT (37 bytes)" 60
wait-serial "000025 (v00 VEDA   TESTNHLT 00000001 VEDA 00000001)" 60
wait-serial "drivervm: Linux powered the machine off" 60
reject-serial "is not a whole table"
